📌 Version: Sealed classes → Java 15 (preview) → Java 17 (standard).

🔹 What is a Sealed Class?
A sealed class/interface lets you restrict which types can extend or implement it.

Normally: any class can extend another (if not final).
With sealed: you explicitly list allowed subclasses → controlled inheritance.

vs final:
  final  → 0 subclasses allowed
  sealed → ONLY the listed subclasses allowed (finite, known set)


🔹 Syntax

public sealed class Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Square {
    // common fields & methods — often abstract sealed class
}

final class Circle extends Shape {
    // final → cannot be extended further
}

final class Rectangle extends Shape {
    // final → cannot be extended further
}

non-sealed class Square extends Shape {
    // non-sealed → anyone can extend Square again
}


🔹 Rules (interview-important)
1. Sealed type must declare permitted subtypes with `permits`
   (Exception: agar saari subclasses SAME file mein hain → `permits` optional, compiler infer kar leta hai)

2. Every permitted subtype MUST be in the same module
   (agar no module → same package)

3. Har subclass MUST declare itself as ONE of:
   - final      → inheritance band
   - sealed     → inheritance further restrict (apna permits likho)
   - non-sealed → inheritance dobara open

4. Subclass must DIRECTLY extend/implement the sealed type
   (indirect extend allowed nahi bina middle type ke permitted hone ke)

5. Sealed class khud abstract ho sakti hai (common pattern)


🔹 Same-file shortcut (permits skip)

public sealed class Shape {          // no permits — same file
    final class Circle extends Shape {}
    final class Rectangle extends Shape {}
}


🔹 Example with Interfaces

public sealed interface Vehicle permits Car, Bike {}

final class Car implements Vehicle {}
final class Bike implements Vehicle {}


🔹 Nested sealed (further restrict)

public sealed class Shape permits Polygon, Circle {}

public sealed class Polygon extends Shape permits Triangle, Rectangle {}
// Polygon sealed → sirf Triangle, Rectangle extend kar sakte hain

public final class Triangle extends Polygon {}
public final class Rectangle extends Polygon {}
public final class Circle extends Shape {}


🔹 Why use Sealed Classes?
✔ Domain modeling — only valid subtypes exist (e.g. Result = Success | Failure)
✔ Exhaustive switch — compiler jaanta hai saari subtypes → default optional
✔ Safer than open inheritance; more flexible than enum (har subtype alag fields/behavior)


🔹 Example: Pattern Matching + exhaustive switch

static String getShapeType(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle c    -> "It's a Circle";
        case Rectangle r -> "It's a Rectangle";
        case Square s    -> "It's a Square";
        // no default needed — sealed hierarchy complete
    };
}

👉 Nayi subtype add ki (permits mein) aur switch update nahi kiya → compile error.
   Yeh intentional safety hai.


⚡ Modifier cheat-sheet

sealed     → restricts inheritance (permits list)
final      → closes inheritance (no children)
non-sealed → re-opens inheritance (anyone can extend)


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Sealed classes give controlled inheritance: only permitted subtypes can extend them, enabling exhaustive pattern-matching switches without a default.”
